\title{Graph Realizations of Closure Orders, Bruhat Symmetry,\\and a Clique-Shadow Bridge}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a general mechanism for representing an ordered parameter space inside a componentwise product without altering its closure structure. Let $(X,\preccurlyeq)$ be a relational space and let $\iota:X\to X$ preserve and reflect the relation. The graph map $\Gamma(x)=(x,\iota(x))$ is injective, realizes $\preccurlyeq$ exactly as the restriction of the product relation, identifies principal lower sets with product-principal lower sets supported on $\Gamma(X)$, and transports arbitrary lower sets exactly. For preorders, inclusion among graph-supported principal closures is equivalent to componentwise comparison.

We specialize this mechanism to Bruhat order on permutations, defined by the Ehresmann rank criterion. Rank matrices determine permutations; the identity and reversal are the minimum and maximum; and the transpose identity $r_{w^{-1}}(i,j)=r_w(j,i)$ gives inversion invariance. Consequently, $w\mapsto(w,w^{-1})$ preserves and reflects Bruhat order and its principal closures. Finally, the two-element shadow of the triangle family of a graph is contained in its edge family. Kruskal--Katona therefore implies that a graph on $n$ vertices with at least $\binom{k}{3}$ triangles, where $3\leq k\leq n$, has at least $\binom{k}{2}$ edges.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A stratified space is often studied through a finite label set equipped with a degeneration relation. If a stratum indexed by $x$ lies in the closure of one indexed by $y$, one writes $x\preccurlyeq y$. In flag-variety settings, permutations serve as labels and Bruhat order governs the expected degeneration pattern. Products of geometric spaces naturally supply multiple projections, so an orbit may be represented by a pair of Weyl-group elements. One must then determine whether geometric closure comparison agrees with componentwise Bruhat comparison on the image of the parametrization.

The order-theoretic issue can be isolated from geometry. A label $x$ is sent to two compatible coordinates, represented by $x$ and $\iota(x)$. If $\iota$ preserves and reflects the relation, its graph is an exact copy of the original ordered space inside the product. This pointwise statement has stronger set-level consequences: principal closures pull back exactly, their intersections with the graph are precisely graph images of original closures, and arbitrary lower sets correspond.

For permutations, the relevant transformation is inversion. Its compatibility with Bruhat order follows from rank matrices: inversion transposes the matrix. Thus $w\mapsto(w,w^{-1})$ realizes Bruhat order as the restriction of product Bruhat order. In applications to orbit closures, this is the combinatorial component; a geometric application additionally requires identification of the orbit closure relation with the Bruhat lower sets.

A second result illustrates shadow methods in extremal graph theory. Triangles form a family of three-element sets, and their lower shadow consists entirely of graph edges. A sharp shadow bound becomes a sharp triangle-to-edge inequality. Both developments follow the same methodological theme: choose a representation in which containment becomes componentwise or set-theoretic, then pull the conclusion back.

\section{Relational graph realizations}

\begin{definition}
Let $X,Y$ carry relations $\preccurlyeq_X,\preccurlyeq_Y$. Their \emph{componentwise product relation} is
\[
(x_1,y_1)\preccurlyeq_\times(x_2,y_2)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
x_1\preccurlyeq_X x_2\ \text{and}\ y_1\preccurlyeq_Y y_2.
\]
For a self-map $\iota:X\to X$, its graph parametrization is
\[
\Gamma(x)=(x,\iota(x)).
\]
For a relation $\preccurlyeq$ on $X$, the principal closure below $x$ is
\[
\down x=\{y\in X:y\preccurlyeq x\}.
\]
A subset $S\subseteq X$ is lower if $y\in S$ and $x\preccurlyeq y$ imply $x\in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Graph realization]
Suppose $\iota:X\to X$ satisfies
\[
\iota(x)\preccurlyeq\iota(y)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\preccurlyeq y
\]
for all $x,y\in X$. Then
\[
\Gamma(x)\preccurlyeq_\times\Gamma(y)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\preccurlyeq y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left side is the conjunction of $x\preccurlyeq y$ and $\iota(x)\preccurlyeq\iota(y)$. The hypothesis makes the two conjuncts equivalent.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity]
For every map $\iota:X\to X$, the graph parametrization is injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equality $\Gamma(x)=\Gamma(y)$ implies equality of first coordinates, so $x=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Principal-closure inclusion]
If $\preccurlyeq$ is reflexive and transitive, then
\[
\down x\subseteq\down y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\preccurlyeq y.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If inclusion holds, reflexivity gives $x\in\down x$, hence $x\in\down y$. Conversely, if $x\preccurlyeq y$ and $z\preccurlyeq x$, transitivity yields $z\preccurlyeq y$.
\end{proof}

Let $\down_\times\Gamma(x)$ denote the principal closure in the product relation.

\begin{theorem}[Exact pullback]
Under the hypotheses of the graph realization theorem,
\[
\Gamma^{-1}(\down_\times\Gamma(x))=\down x.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An element $y$ belongs to the left side precisely when $\Gamma(y)\preccurlyeq_\times\Gamma(x)$, which by graph realization is equivalent to $y\preccurlyeq x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Graph-supported closure identity]
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
\down_\times\Gamma(x)\cap\Gamma(X)=\Gamma(\down x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A point in the intersection has the form $\Gamma(y)$, and product comparison with $\Gamma(x)$ is equivalent to $y\preccurlyeq x$. The reverse inclusion follows from the same equivalence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closure inclusion on the graph]
If $\preccurlyeq$ is reflexive and transitive, then, for any self-map $\iota$,
\[
\down_\times\Gamma(x)\cap\Gamma(X)
\subseteq
\down_\times\Gamma(y)\cap\Gamma(X)
\]
if and only if $\Gamma(x)\preccurlyeq_\times\Gamma(y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the forward direction, reflexivity puts $\Gamma(x)$ in its own graph-supported closure, and inclusion puts it below $\Gamma(y)$. Conversely, any graph point below $\Gamma(x)$ is below $\Gamma(y)$ by coordinatewise transitivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Lower-set correspondence]
Under the graph realization hypotheses, $S\subseteq X$ is lower if and only if $\Gamma(S)$ is lower relative to $\Gamma(X)$ under the product relation. Explicitly, whenever $p,q\in\Gamma(X)$, $p\preccurlyeq_\times q$, and $q\in\Gamma(S)$, one has $p\in\Gamma(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $p=\Gamma(x)$ and $q=\Gamma(y)$. Injectivity makes $q\in\Gamma(S)$ equivalent to $y\in S$, while graph realization makes $p\preccurlyeq_\times q$ equivalent to $x\preccurlyeq y$. The condition is exactly lowerness of $S$.
\end{proof}

\section{Bruhat order through rank matrices}

Let $S_n$ be the symmetric group on $\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$. For $w\in S_n$ define the Ehresmann rank count
\[
r_w(i,j)=\#\{k:0\leq k\leq i\text{ and }w(k)\leq j\}.
\]
It counts the ones in a northwest rectangle of the permutation matrix.

\begin{definition}[Bruhat order]
For $u,v\in S_n$, write $u\bruhat v$ if
\[
r_v(i,j)\leq r_u(i,j)
\]
for every $i,j$. Define product Bruhat order componentwise:
\[
(a,b)\prodbruhat(c,d)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad a\bruhat c\text{ and }b\bruhat d.
\]
\end{definition}

Reflexivity and transitivity are immediate from integer inequalities.

\begin{theorem}[Rank determination and antisymmetry]
If $u\bruhat v$ and $v\bruhat u$, then $u=v$. Hence Bruhat order is a partial order.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The comparisons give $r_u(i,j)=r_v(i,j)$ everywhere. The difference between the count through row $i$ and that through row $i-1$ is the indicator of $w(i)\leq j$. Thus the rank matrix determines every threshold predicate $w(i)\leq j$, which determines $w(i)$. Therefore $u(i)=v(i)$ for every $i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal permutations]
The identity is the minimum of $(S_n,\bruhat)$, and the reversal $w_0(i)=n-1-i$ is the maximum.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the identity, a northwest rectangle contains $\min(i,j)+1$ diagonal entries. Any permutation contributes at most $i+1$ entries by rows and at most $j+1$ by distinct values, so the identity has the maximal rank counts required of the Bruhat minimum.

For reversal,
\[
r_{w_0}(i,j)=\max(0,i+j+2-n).
\]
For any permutation, intersect the $i+1$ positions at most $i$ with the $j+1$ positions whose images are at most $j$. Two subsets of an $n$-element set have intersection of size at least $(i+1)+(j+1)-n$, truncated at zero. Thus $r_{w_0}(i,j)\leq r_w(i,j)$, so $w\bruhat w_0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The inversion set and inversion length are
\[
\operatorname{Inv}(w)=\{(i,j):i<j\text{ and }w(j)<w(i)\},
\qquad \ell(w)=|\operatorname{Inv}(w)|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Zero length]
A permutation has $\ell(w)=0$ if and only if it is the identity.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Zero inversions make $w$ strictly increasing. The only increasing bijection of a finite chain to itself is the identity. The converse is immediate.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Length characterization of the minimum]
A permutation lies below every element of $S_n$ if and only if its inversion length is zero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
If $w$ lies below every element, it lies below the identity; the identity also lies below $w$, so antisymmetry gives $w=\mathrm{id}$. The converse follows from minimality of the identity.
\end{proof}

\section{Inversion and the two-projection closure law}

\begin{lemma}[Transpose identity]
For every $w\in S_n$,
\[
r_{w^{-1}}(i,j)=r_w(j,i).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The left side counts values $k\leq i$ whose preimages obey $w^{-1}(k)\leq j$. Sending $k$ to $w^{-1}(k)$ bijects this set with positions $a\leq j$ for which $w(a)\leq i$. Equivalently, inversion transposes the permutation matrix.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inversion invariance]
For all $u,v\in S_n$,
\[
u\bruhat v\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad u^{-1}\bruhat v^{-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exchange $i$ and $j$ in all defining inequalities and apply the transpose identity. The reverse direction is identical because inversion is involutive.
\end{proof}

Define $\Phi:S_n\to S_n\times S_n$ by $\Phi(w)=(w,w^{-1})$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-projection Bruhat embedding]
The map $\Phi$ is injective and
\[
u\bruhat v\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\Phi(u)\prodbruhat\Phi(v).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first coordinate gives injectivity. Product comparison asks for both $u\bruhat v$ and $u^{-1}\bruhat v^{-1}$, which are equivalent by inversion invariance.
\end{proof}

For $w\in S_n$, let $C(w)=\{u:u\bruhat w\}$, and let $C_\times(\Phi(w))$ be the product-principal closure.

\begin{corollary}[Exact closure correspondence]
For every $w\in S_n$,
\[
\Phi^{-1}(C_\times(\Phi(w)))=C(w),
\qquad
C_\times(\Phi(w))\cap\Phi(S_n)=\Phi(C(w)).
\]
Furthermore, inclusion between the graph-supported product closures below $\Phi(u)$ and $\Phi(v)$ is equivalent to $\Phi(u)\prodbruhat\Phi(v)$, and hence to $u\bruhat v$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the exact pullback, graph-supported closure, and closure-inclusion theorems to Bruhat order and inversion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bruhat lower sets on the image]
A subset $S\subseteq S_n$ is Bruhat-lower if and only if $\Phi(S)$ is product-Bruhat-lower relative to $\Phi(S_n)$.
\end{corollary}

This is the precise order-theoretic content needed for a two-projection parametrization of strata. If a geometric classification identifies strata with $S_n$ and geometric closure with principal Bruhat lower sets, then closure inclusion is equivalent to componentwise Bruhat comparison of the two projection labels. The theorem supplies the combinatorial mechanism; the geometric identification remains a separate hypothesis.

\section{A Kruskal--Katona bridge from triangles to edges}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph with $|V|=n$, and let $\mathcal T(G)$ be its family of triangles, regarded as three-element subsets of $V$.

\begin{definition}[Lower shadow]
For a family $\mathcal F$ of $r$-element sets, define
\[
\partial\mathcal F=\{A:|A|=r-1\text{ and }A\subset B\text{ for some }B\in\mathcal F\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Triangle shadow]
For every finite simple graph,
\[
\partial\mathcal T(G)\subseteq E,
\]
where an edge is identified with its two-element endpoint set.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
A shadow member is obtained by deleting one vertex from a triangle. The two remaining vertices are adjacent.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform shadow bound]
Let $\mathcal F$ be a family of three-element subsets of an $n$-element set. If $3\leq k\leq n$ and
\[
|\mathcal F|\geq\binom{k}{3},
\]
then
\[
|\partial\mathcal F|\geq\binom{k}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Kruskal--Katona theorem says that initial colexicographic segments minimize the lower shadow among uniform families of fixed size. At size $\binom{k}{3}$, the extremal family consists of all triples from a fixed $k$-element set. Its shadow consists of all pairs from that set and has size $\binom{k}{2}$. Adding triples cannot decrease a shadow.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Triangle-edge inequality]
Let $G$ be a simple graph on $n$ vertices. If $3\leq k\leq n$ and $G$ has at least $\binom{k}{3}$ triangles, then $G$ has at least $\binom{k}{2}$ edges.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the uniform shadow bound to $\mathcal T(G)$ and use the triangle-shadow inclusion:
\[
\binom{k}{2}\leq|\partial\mathcal T(G)|\leq|E|.
\]
\end{proof}

The theorem is sharp. A complete graph on $k$ vertices together with $n-k$ isolated vertices has exactly $\binom{k}{3}$ triangles and $\binom{k}{2}$ edges.

\section{Algorithms and numerical illustrations}

A direct Bruhat comparison constructs $r_u$ and $r_v$ and checks $r_v(i,j)\leq r_u(i,j)$ for all $i,j$. Counting separately in each cell costs $O(n^3)$ time. Constructing a permutation matrix and taking two-dimensional prefix sums reduces the cost to $O(n^2)$ time and $O(n^2)$ storage. Inversion is computable in $O(n)$ time.

For $w=[2,0,3,1]$, one has $w^{-1}=[1,3,0,2]$. Their rank matrices satisfy the transpose identity cell by cell. For finite $S_n$, a principal closure below $x$ is computed by enumerating every $y$ and retaining those with $y\bruhat x$. Testing both coordinates retains exactly the same labels.

For graph shadows, enumerate triples $a<b<c$ and test all three adjacencies. Insert each triangle's three two-element faces into a set. This costs $O(n^3)$ adjacency tests. For $K_6$,
\[
|\mathcal T(K_6)|=\binom63=20,
\qquad
|\partial\mathcal T(K_6)|=|E(K_6)|=\binom62=15.
\]
Likewise, $K_5$ plus isolated vertices has ten triangles and ten edges, attaining equality at $k=5$.

\section{Applications, limitations, and future work}

The graph realization applies whenever a classification supplies a label and a compatible transformed label. For two projections from a product of flag spaces, a graph-form image with inversion as second coordinate makes componentwise comparison exact on that image. The lower-set theorem treats closed or degeneration-stable unions, and offers a consistency test for computational closure tables.

The order-theoretic results do not alone identify Zariski closures of Borel orbits. Such an application must construct the orbit parametrization and prove that its geometric closure relation is the Bruhat relation considered here. More general parametrizations may also use two different target posets; then the combined coordinate map, rather than either coordinate alone, should be required to preserve and reflect order.

The triangle-edge result is a binomial-threshold theorem, not the full optimal formula for every triangle count. The full binomial representation in Kruskal--Katona gives finer bounds. Stability statements describing all graphs near equality also require further arguments.

Natural next steps are: to instantiate the graph-supported closure statements in one unified rank-matrix treatment; to connect principal lower sets to actual Zariski closures after developing the required flag-variety geometry; to allow two distinct order-reflecting coordinate maps into different Weyl groups; and to prove a lattice isomorphism between Bruhat lower sets and product-order lower subsets supported on the parametrization image. In extremal graph theory, replacing triangles by $r$-cliques yields a hierarchy: the $(r-1)$-shadow of the $r$-clique family lies in the family of $(r-1)$-cliques.

\section{Conclusion}

A relation-preserving and relation-reflecting transformation turns its graph into an exact ordered copy of the original space inside a product. This controls principal closures, closure inclusion, and all lower sets. Permutation inversion provides a geometrically relevant instance because it transposes Ehresmann rank matrices, making $w\mapsto(w,w^{-1})$ an exact Bruhat embedding.

The graph-theoretic theorem gives a parallel lesson: every two-element shadow of a triangle is an edge, and a sharp uniform-family bound converts triangle abundance into an edge lower bound. In both settings, a difficult structural question becomes transparent after choosing a representation adapted to containment.

